# Kick the first-boot timezone autodetection when an interface actually gets an
# address (MiSTer -- see ADR 0025 and etc/init.d/S48timezone).
#
# WHY A HOOK AT ALL. S48timezone runs at S48, seconds after dhcpcd starts, and
# it can only wait so long before it has to let go. But the case that actually
# matters is not "the lease was slow" -- it is "there was no network on the
# first boot at all". A MiSTer card is flashed, booted, and only *then* does the
# user set up wpa_supplicant.conf, so the first boot is routinely offline and
# every boot after it is not. Polling harder at S48 cannot fix that; being told
# when an address arrives can, and dhcpcd already offers exactly that. This is
# the same extension point dhcpcd's own 20-resolv.conf and 30-hostname use.
#
# SAFE TO FIRE OFTEN. `S48timezone start` is a no-op once a timezone is set or
# the guess has been spent, it takes its own lock so two lookups cannot overlap,
# and it backgrounds the work -- so this never delays dhcpcd, and a RENEW every
# few hours costs three stat() calls, not a query.
#
# Sourced, not executed (dhcpcd-run-hooks: `. "$hook"`), like every hook in this
# directory: hence no shebang, no exec bit, and no `exit` -- an `exit` here would
# end dhcpcd's whole hook run, taking 20-resolv.conf and 30-hostname with it.

# NB: dhcpcd's own hooks write this as `if $if_configured && $if_up`, i.e. they
# EXECUTE the variable as a command. That is their idiom and it works, because
# dhcpcd sets these to the literal strings true/false. It is compared as data
# here anyway: this file is sourced into dhcpcd's shell, and a variable that is
# unset, empty, or ever anything other than those two strings would then be run
# as a command. Nothing reaches if_up from the network, so this is not a hole
# being closed -- it is one that never gets to open.
case "$reason" in
BOUND|REBOOT|REBIND|RENEW|BOUND6|REBOOT6|REBIND6|RENEW6)
	if [ "${if_up:-false}" = true ] && [ -x /etc/init.d/S48timezone ]; then
		/etc/init.d/S48timezone start
	fi
	;;
esac
